---
import CalcPage from '../components/CalcPage.astro';
import def from '../calculators/btu';
import { ENERGY_STAR_BTU } from '../lib/hvac';

const rows = ENERGY_STAR_BTU.map(([max, btu], i) => ({ from: i === 0 ? 100 : ENERGY_STAR_BTU[i - 1][0], max, btu }));
---

<CalcPage
  slug="btu-calculator"
  def={def}
  title="BTU Calculator: Air Conditioner Size for a Room"
  description="Free BTU calculator. Enter room size, sun exposure, occupants, and whether it's a kitchen to get the air conditioner BTUs and tons you need, using the ENERGY STAR sizing method."
  h1="BTU Calculator"
  updated="2026-11-21"
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Estimate the cooling capacity for a room air conditioner using the ENERGY STAR sizing chart. Enter the room
    dimensions or floor area, then adjust for sunlight, occupants, and kitchen use.
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="how-to">How many BTUs do I need?</h2>
  <p>
    Find the room's base capacity in the table below. For a <strong>20 × 20 ft room with an 8 ft ceiling</strong>,
    the 400 ft² base is <strong>9,000 BTU/h</strong>. If that room gets strong sun and usually has four people,
    add 900 BTU/h for sunlight and 1,200 BTU/h for the two extra people. The estimate comes to <strong>11,100 BTU/h</strong>.
    Compare that result with the rated cooling capacity on the models you are considering.
  </p>

  <h2 id="table">Air conditioner size by room size</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="https://www.energystar.gov/products/room_air_conditioners">ENERGY STAR room air conditioner sizing chart</a>
    covers 100–2,500 ft² and assumes an 8 ft ceiling. At a shared cutoff, the calculator uses the lower band.
    For example, exactly 400 ft² uses 9,000 BTU/h, and an area just above 400 ft² uses 10,000 BTU/h.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Room area</th><th class="num">Cooling capacity</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{rows.map((r) => <tr><td>{r.from}–{r.max.toLocaleString('en-US')} sq ft</td><td class="num">{r.btu.toLocaleString('en-US')} BTU/h</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    The calculator takes off 10% for heavy shade, adds 10% for strong sun, adds 600 BTU/h for each regular occupant
    beyond two, and adds 4,000 BTU/h for a kitchen. For a room with a high ceiling, confirm the size with the
    manufacturer or installer before buying.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tons">BTUs and tons</h2>
  <p>
    Central air and heat pumps are sold in tons. One ton of cooling is 12,000 BTU per hour, so a 24,000 BTU system is 2
    tons. Room units are usually labeled in BTU.
  </p>

  <h2 id="heating">How many BTUs to heat a room?</h2>
  <p>
    A rough rule is 30 to 60 BTU per square foot, higher in colder climates and poorly insulated homes. The calculator
    shows a range for the climate you pick. Treat it as a starting point for space heaters and small zones, not for a
    furnace.
  </p>

  <p>
    L-shaped room or open plan? <a href="/square-footage-calculator/">Add up the floor area</a> first and enter the total.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>Is a bigger air conditioner better?</h3>
  <p>
    No. An oversized unit cools the air quickly and shuts off before it removes much humidity, leaving the room cold and
    damp, and it cycles more often. Choose the size that matches the room.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I size a mini-split or central system with this chart?</h3>
  <p>
    Ask the installer for an <a href="https://www.acca.org/standards/technical-manuals/manual-j">ACCA Manual J load calculation</a>
    for the rooms the system will serve. It accounts for factors such as insulation, windows, and air leakage that the
    room air conditioner chart does not model.
  </p>
  <p>
    Improving the room instead? Better attic insulation cuts the load; see the
    <a href="/insulation-calculator/">insulation calculator</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="method">How this calculator works</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Below 100 ft², the calculator uses the smallest cooling band. Above 2,500 ft², it extrapolates the chart's final band.</li>
    <li>The separate heating estimate multiplies floor area by a rough climate-based range of 30–60 BTU/h per ft².</li>
  </ul>
</CalcPage>
